    "img": "/decks/hs-day4/37.webp",
    "title": "12-Month Timeframe",
    "section": "Medicare",
    "text": [
     "If the provider or hospital is not paid, or if the claim is not resolved (e.g., through settlement) within 12 months, and they opt not to bill Medicare, they must cease all collection efforts against both the patient and the liability carrier.",
     "Providers have 12 months from the date of service to bill Medicare. This period is referred to as Medicare’s timely filing requirement."
    ],
    "from": "hs-day4, slide 38",
    "handout": "",
    "notes": "Second, the twelve-month timeframe. If the provider is not paid, or the claim is not resolved, for example through settlement, within twelve months, and the provider opted not to bill Medicare, it must stop all collection efforts against both the patient and the liability carrier. Providers have twelve months from the date of service to bill Medicare, and that is called Medicare's timely filing requirement."
