    "from": "hs-day4, slide 45",
    "handout": "",
    "notes": "Part three is Medicaid. Medicaid is different from Medicare in who it covers, who runs it and how its liens are handled. Because each state administers its own program, the rules and contacts vary. We will cover what Medicaid is, its subrogation rights, an example process, and some practical tips for settling Medicaid liens, particularly in Nevada. Medicaid is a government insurance program for people of all ages whose income and resources are not enough to cover healthcare costs. The picture shows a sample Nevada Medicaid card, so you know what to look for in the client's documents, such as the recipient number. When a client mentions Medicaid, ask for a copy of the card at intake so the claim can be opened early. Medicaid operates differently in each state. It is managed by state and local governments, while following federal regulations. Also note that patients without health insurance can apply for Medicaid at the hospital if they meet the income criteria. So a client who had no insurance on the day of the accident may still turn out to have Medicaid paying for treatment. Always ask."
